#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111604} :activity/id #uuid "78489629-e548-5866-8b96-e96990be5e28" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048295 :topic/id #uuid "920e0459-5102-56be-8b6e-f637c62f1366" :topic/math-academy-id 1531} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111827 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of ![](images/d0/d0a7418e3803197d07b85f03b206b83bed1c4d451397ae953b05634db9a9f829.svg) First, we create a table of values: \n\n| ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) | ![](images/78/78d4465d85b55cee3832a9b598a2564b6a3fa9a072de8a1fb4b40ca884079670.svg) | ![](images/63/63df422d619ca962ca5a69bc7d25dd8831269b92fa5d641dcb960819d3868a9a.svg) | ![](images/66/66ef4b5796888b4025ee2d8b3d516ec80741b341db9389696b6e77a4af5208d8.svg) | ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) | ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/e5/e51853c53dfd3d72f8fd380044f7ce864974b3d8c1ca0e690b7caba34fe3456c.svg) | ![](images/c7/c700188055bde6c02303571bfb6b737fddda4a14c08edf1cdeeaf684530eea88.svg) | ![](images/40/402ff5d0c0c3aaa47f18c89f6d96c95d47840eac13822d596a51bdb0c5f9a4ab.svg) | ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/0b/0b47cf9f83219ef6bab2ac82c70ded537ce9db2ab3945b85d5ca678f16b30a75.svg) | ![](images/fc/fce859f2a3c5348530c75d1a07cf8bb4ff0a228bdf8d1e493294b4489b84fdee.svg) | ![](images/8a/8ab6a43799989375f441258e25082fb84a876ad9273de9dba1bc200dca195381.svg) |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/b0/b045833e2e37e2ed4c35e9c8abe8c379dfd7de7eded17d4dc943205677d7fce5.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1\\. The graph has a ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept at ![](images/fd/fd83db97b0675d3527a9f2a7a68d38a98f2d05ab5b1771d961c0d687e44c191b.svg)\n\n2\\. The graph is decreasing.\n\n3\\. For ![](images/e4/e45cbcbc2e3f466cab4e379776bce2640286bc2a918ff0177c0433b97f7cd4b7.svg), the graph grows very rapidly as ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) decreases. Symbolically, we can write ![](images/f5/f54328d6cf80c554acae5f7a9693b0eb4ea745ec0a4b0f5013b03a3e41ae27db.svg) as ![](images/72/7248bd0778a7746f6b1018c7bc926d8e678ba829b741beed092d802bd7ba1233.svg)\n\n4\\. For ![](images/ee/ee98d74fc058e95e72d13a5e575e70e4d5adedd26413e3f01ab1e3f788be87e8.svg), the graph gets very close to zero as ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) increases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has a horizontal asymptote ![](images/6c/6c63827e94b6c11a9803605bd54156d90d3fcc6ff4639dd179a5fb024bcffaa1.svg) Symbolically, we can write ![](images/43/43a6d025efce6b330ba7907d1daa54ad58cf04feee07adedad66d4c806b5f88d.svg) as ![](images/f9/f9b318487299434d45096742eaad8ea148abb88c27b718c1bc0983f927dc9911.svg)\n\n5\\. The domain is ![](images/04/043681a7084203dcb8609f78989cadb8f0bcd701978361940d93ef850da95a14.svg), and the range is ![](images/95/95d0bc38cd8737723521d2a1846cb71a0dba33d3df26bd73c18acbb650fc8b95.svg)\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve ![](images/90/90517bb7bc12cfec9d00d88592bbda7572f4a0c5cbcf4f66049311d42f20f6ca.svg) with ![](images/e2/e276af13a21df7f4755de7b18da1a10ca590bbdf3387515efa0896f6cddb79cf.svg) These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/0f/0f8e7cc0db94f5f51bc83404351a7708171fdce50e2946b51263885e4230ecef.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6e919c9a-e896-5b0b-8570-5a7a377146d3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1544 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fa05b5ee-1d45-5481-a6c9-e019dfaa173b" :step/math-academy-id 3935 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111709}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110313 :question/id #uuid "c02ce0b7-d660-5d31-8147-303a9e6f806d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1223"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508088}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"} :step/id #uuid "41927360-32e5-5db6-a9f3-26f71dd06db5" :step/math-academy-id 3936 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111828 :tutorial/content "Multiplying an exponential decay function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve ![](images/d5/d59881254ee1a66445dcf2697e8af4426cbe9d219443d5f40ef2aefc426d598b.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/fb/fb1255ff2ad78ef15b03968cb806543d4f29f1029680068f76c913bb4cab96ad.svg) by a factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\nIt takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/fb/fb1255ff2ad78ef15b03968cb806543d4f29f1029680068f76c913bb4cab96ad.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/d5/d5cf96822f65e51d618b651174f1c516eb97be3bf61f5b1380d1c245d29a5a72.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/43/437619dfcf8db5be350de504dc9d98eab13c2ac21c62998d3c268a1f475a30d9.svg)\n\nOn the other hand, the curve\n![](images/99/99f750a011acdbe86e9c9397d5e5901c686a2c10b625fa9dc98a0b90a516c592.svg)\nrepresents a vertical scaling of\n![](images/c8/c8c077b168d3c60c74b354ee942893abefb51b5e08b7483044ea5ea2edf50795.svg)\nby a factor of ![](images/31/31659384627c9aa2540de6fca01634acdcdd4c77dd705fbf922c813cc23e822f.svg) It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/c8/c8c077b168d3c60c74b354ee942893abefb51b5e08b7483044ea5ea2edf50795.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/31/31659384627c9aa2540de6fca01634acdcdd4c77dd705fbf922c813cc23e822f.svg) This has the effect of **compressing** the curve downwards.\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched or compressed exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ea28190-d93f-521e-964b-cdd4aad84f69" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1545 :tutorial/title "Vertical Stretching and Compression"} :step/id #uuid "04a9b12e-b969-57f1-a5e7-19bc2fc561a7" :step/math-academy-id 3939 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110668 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110314 :question/id #uuid "90054a4d-adcf-56b8-ab73-86048bc0f4d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-1224"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46b4837f-6ba5-5812-8dc7-4671fcd6b20d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557244}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Scaled Exponential Decay Function"} :step/id #uuid "adbf259f-fa2f-5745-bd7e-7048bd565045" :step/math-academy-id 3940 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110315 :question/id #uuid "055bdf27-d354-57d7-96a6-de3300053035" :question/math-academy-id "e-1225"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34645eb8-3624-5ff3-817d-28f8d0cee4ba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557217}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Scaling Constant From a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "9dfe9d61-57bc-5cba-b15e-a9df1319f56e" :step/math-academy-id 3943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110736 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110370 :question/id #uuid "edf79f97-9325-55a0-a78b-133bba524177" :question/math-academy-id "e-1803"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41d2e6d0-bfad-5fe0-a055-aaa9ed9ae31c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508104}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of an Exponential Decay Function"} :step/id #uuid "2cc2ead9-f661-50ca-96a5-5747e77f1192" :step/math-academy-id 6235 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111605}}] :activity/title "Graphing Exponential Decay Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110876}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557357} :activity/id #uuid "0c2481e8-aee3-5235-9ea1-adc64f59f5cc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048295 :topic/id #uuid "920e0459-5102-56be-8b6e-f637c62f1366" :topic/math-academy-id 1531} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4933cab4-6c6f-5e1e-b9e9-375a48b8bbd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1c/1c7ef699169e521845d96c6b28392271c27cca116b449277c66817dc2dbb6491.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390342} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd7f842-ec45-57a8-93f4-8edce627ac5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/33/3357b2cf67b40afa54b081c67855f28a74d71209459382c0e421b6e2da7d114f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390343} {:answer/id #uuid "c26f47ff-1aa3-50e2-840e-6408b7c4640f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/dc/dcec9bbf7c5a1badb7e0193a16e802fe0b5558c1a076f65da60db5a24a58f667.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390344} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0bbddd-f61f-57da-9b31-1e4e5fbfbe90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/04/04899589bc5527f5c35bae7d2b8564141ea5ae49f0aab1f500e57be7d854cc05.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390345} {:answer/id #uuid "3bdb4a02-ebd9-56ca-9cfc-e2db41cc39a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d3/d3d7ea5224600381e2dd4f62618ce8a60804f89bb6949b82989017aa2556f87a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390346}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390345} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff039e87-4af3-51fe-96a4-b9dddf2e393f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "09d1de29-e974-591a-b590-9887d91abf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-18523" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=2{(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{6})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/4d/4dacecba4626f7c202777cad0a723295a2954f4b0c65fde83a81c6c849414d51.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $2.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,2),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/b7/b747ae8c29f01b840930d81f9c4b706d049ca685ee982efff717ddb13213937d.png)"} :step/id #uuid "42fbdbbd-fd40-5071-babd-f696e3b97b50" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557381}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557364 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c4c61c-51ad-50d2-aac0-fb3d0ca8ba37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/37/374572e9b9313502b6d806d37b095320496dc65d4e6383ea509b3ec30bc1aa85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556485} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb02bbf-e4db-51d2-820e-c820cd6c3132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a3/a31cc9740f5db8ae825021de611c67b8114c3a5bffb13aca95b973b3718e8bd5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556493} {:answer/id #uuid "2307cbd4-8b2c-507e-9d61-ab458dea3b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/bf/bfe6c167ff292ffd0b84926308734dc23888de3ba3cda5018d2c8fd24ec3436e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556509} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaf05ab-28d2-5ec9-bcfe-eb752c46cfe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/76/76d3aa6e8e8dd2ccdad2784f664ba915efceef5573e71a389ab72f1e36aba106.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556919} {:answer/id #uuid "d5cc661e-81a0-5227-89bf-6972cafbeab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/da/dad79e84e25ce963588ee32b0da65a21c2fd70faad464b2ec2d3ce1a09c60ed9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556949}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556485} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4b796fd-2386-5360-a29c-d6f97f457e1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "487e5162-7f53-555d-bd23-288509fce232" :question/math-academy-id "q-27526" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{8})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/f5/f5d15cae5283eb02d555bee57f399dd00c58ec319413ca5817b1edba1ac69fb9.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{2}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b8e997035826ffcb6969c5bbdda46e256234fb5720f45aa281719be631929d4.png)"} :step/id #uuid "6e940ef1-cb74-5ed5-96e9-fd160e8a8602"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557381 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557217 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29951222-87d7-548a-9a5e-144857424fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556519} {:answer/id #uuid "46324b89-8e6d-58d2-acb1-a3d8251446db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556585} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff11515-d97e-5995-80f0-2ad0af75b6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556820} {:answer/id #uuid "b92f6a22-272b-56d9-abd2-b059ce0496cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556882} {:answer/id #uuid "e276d83b-7cf3-5d6b-86cd-b9f78e932f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186556820} :answer-field/id #uuid "50488e0d-3249-550c-8d98-bec9f2011aab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "228ddf28-6449-527a-a0f3-502e03cb4eba" :question/math-academy-id "q-18525" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9738ccf12d8b9e8f3ed84870371972767e67a624f31fe5782f39679253965c4.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x},$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,4)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=4.$"} :step/id #uuid "9711648c-65ca-5e78-b9ef-2265fc45848c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557364}}] :activity/title "Graphing Exponential Decay Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556408}]}
